# Client-side build configuration.
#
# TURN credentials are deliberately NOT here any more. They are short-lived, and
# a pair baked into the bundle expires mid-session — which breaks the follower's
# reconnect in a way that looks exactly like the Android disconnection bug the
# relay exists to fix. Credentials are minted per client by the Worker
# (worker/index.ts) and fetched at join time from /api/ice.
#
# The Worker's secrets live in Cloudflare, never in this repo:
#
#   yarn wrangler secret put TURN_KEY_ID
#   yarn wrangler secret put TURN_KEY_API_TOKEN
#
# For LOCAL development you do not need either of those, and you do not need to
# log in to Cloudflare at all. Put the same two values in a `.dev.vars` file in
# the repo root (gitignored) and `wrangler dev` reads them straight from disk:
#
# Use `.dev.vars`, NOT this file or `.env.local`. Wrangler will happily read
# those too, but Vite reads them as well — and Vite inlines anything prefixed
# `VITE_` into the client bundle. Keeping the TURN API token in a file Vite
# never opens means a stray prefix cannot ship an account-billable secret to
# every visitor. Cloudflare also treats the two as exclusive: if `.dev.vars`
# exists, `.env` values are dropped from the Worker's env entirely.
#
#   TURN_KEY_ID=...
#   TURN_KEY_API_TOKEN=...
#
# Then run the app and the Worker side by side — Vite proxies /api to it:
#
#   yarn dev          # :3100
#   yarn worker:dev   # :8787

# Trystero matchmaking backend: nostr (default), mqtt or torrent.
# VITE_TRYSTERO_STRATEGY=nostr

# Comma-separated Nostr relay URLs, overriding Trystero's defaults.
# VITE_NOSTR_RELAYS=
